WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:07.100
NIKHIL KOTHARI: Hello, and
welcome to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.080
live webinar from Google Cloud.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.610
I'm Nikhil Kothari,
an engineering

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:13.880
lead on Google Cloud Platform,
leading the ML Engine Team.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.430
We're hosting these live
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:18.920
Throughout this talk, you
can enter your questions.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.500
And my colleague,
Dinesh Kulkarni,

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.670
who's a project manager on the
team, will be able to answer.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:30.090
We'll also take some live
Q&amp;A at the end of this talk.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:34.870
So with that, let's get
started with the presentation.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:37.660
Machine learning is all
around us these days.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.220
Data scientists
and developers are

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.572
doing some really creative
things with their data

00:00:41.572 --> 00:00:43.780
and powering some really
interesting machine learning

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.502
use cases.

00:00:45.502 --> 00:00:46.960
One way to look at
machine learning

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.790
is through the lens
of the data that you

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:53.410
might use for your problem.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:57.610
And this data might be
unstructured data, like images,

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:00.070
or it might be the more
familiar, structured, tabular

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:01.390
data.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:05.200
And new and upcoming is also
time series and sequential

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:05.885
data.

00:01:05.885 --> 00:01:07.510
Another way to look
at machine learning

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:09.580
is the business problems
that you might tackle.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.365
And these might range from
perception and understanding

00:01:12.365 --> 00:01:16.150
of all of this content,
to powering search,

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.190
to doing things
like recommendation,

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:22.930
as well as estimation of price
and risk in various businesses,

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:24.790
or things like
anomaly detection,

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:27.910
fraud detection, and so on.

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:31.390
Cloud ML Engine allows you to
tackle your machine learning

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:35.770
use cases with your data.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:40.280
ML Engine in a nutshell is
about TensorFlow as a service.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.520
It allows you to do training
and prediction, which

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:46.270
are the two most important
aspects of machine learning.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:48.190
As a managed
service, it provides

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:52.560
APIs for these two
building blocks, training

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.210
and prediction, and allows
you to bring your data

00:01:55.210 --> 00:02:00.340
and your models to the problem.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.470
Taking a deeper
slightly deeper look

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:04.480
at these two parts
of the product,

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.660
training is all about bringing
to you the capabilities

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.470
to build your best model
at the best performance.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:13.950
Using TensorFlow, you
can avail yourself

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.540
of scalable and
distributed training

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.730
that scales to the
largest of data sets.

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:21.520
You can use
accelerators like GPUs,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.290
and soon, TPUs, to get the
best performance and quicker

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:28.750
turnaround time for
your experiments.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:30.460
And then we also
offer capabilities

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.830
like HyperTune, which is
a hyper-parameter tuning

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:36.520
capability to allow you to find
and optimize your parameter

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.760
space for your best model.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.560
The training API takes care
of all the infrastructure,

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.240
whether it's compute resources
or things like your jobs,

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.140
as well as the software stack
in terms of all the packages

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:54.790
and drivers and environment
needed for doing training.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.430
On the prediction side,
once you have a model,

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:02.020
it's about bringing to you
the best production experience

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.260
without having you to
take on all the developer

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:10.067
operations in hosting and
serving models online.

00:03:10.067 --> 00:03:11.650
It starts with model
management, which

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.930
allows you to bring
in models, deploy them

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.600
along with different
versions of your models.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.180
And it constructs
an HTTP endpoint

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:22.315
that you can use
to connect securely

00:03:22.315 --> 00:03:24.730
to your model and issue
requests and responses

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:25.900
to get predictions.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:30.040
You can also use these models
in batch processing pipelines

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:32.790
using batch prediction.

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:38.050
I'll touch on a few of these in
the demo as we switch to that.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:41.110
I'll be demoing a
very high-level view

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:44.855
of ML Engine using Jupyter
Notebooks, using a product

00:03:44.855 --> 00:03:45.730
called Cloud Datalab.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:50.122
So this is Cloud Datalab.

00:03:50.122 --> 00:03:51.580
I've already opened
it up, and I've

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:55.330
got a variety of notebooks.

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:58.390
Like any ML problem,
it starts with data.

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:00.250
It starts with
understanding your data.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:04.420
And the notebook format
allows you to very quickly get

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:05.464
a sense of your data.

00:04:05.464 --> 00:04:06.880
In this particular
case, I'm going

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:10.710
to be using the New
York taxi fare data

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:15.670
set, which contains data
about different taxi rides--

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:18.690
their pick-up points, and
drop-off off points, and fares.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.377
And what we're going
to do is build a model

00:04:21.377 --> 00:04:22.210
to predict the fare.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:28.299
And all this data
is in BigQuery.

00:04:28.299 --> 00:04:30.090
So the first thing you
might do is actually

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:31.260
understanding your data.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:34.440
And this involves first
looking at the schema, which

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.220
you can see using these tools
that we have in Datalab--

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:42.210
things like %bq to work
with BitQuery APIs.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.460
So I can get a
sense of my schema.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.380
I can also get a
sense of my data.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.741
So this thing here has--

00:04:48.741 --> 00:04:50.350
it's a fairly large data set.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.590
So clearly, I'm also going
to be doing some sampling

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:57.330
to be able to work with
it in an interactive way.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.420
Here's a quick
query that pulls out

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:02.600
locations from this data set.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:06.330
And for every 10,000 rows,
it pulls out a data point.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:08.750
So that's my simple sampling.

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:10.400
And then I can go
ahead and plot it.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.860
And I get a fairly
uninteresting plot.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:14.360
But it's actually interesting.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:20.001
A bunch of data points are at
the 0 comma 0-- lat/long 0.

00:05:20.001 --> 00:05:22.250
So clearly, there's some
data cleanup to be also done,

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:23.707
and filtering.

00:05:23.707 --> 00:05:25.040
And so what I can do from what--

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:26.610
I know that this
is about New York.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:31.340
And so I can sort of bound
the lat/long pairs to New York

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:34.900
and filter out all the data
that falls out of that.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.785
And once I've done that
with a simple WHERE

00:05:36.785 --> 00:05:40.740
clause that filters
data to New York,

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.700
I can re-execute this query.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:45.180
I can execute that SQL.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.180
It executes in BigQuery.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.250
I retrieve that as
a pandas DataFrame.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:51.200
Many of you are probably
familiar with pandas.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.840
And then I can very
easily plot it.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:57.454
And those who are familiar with
the geography around New York

00:05:57.454 --> 00:05:58.620
probably can recognize this.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.280
This sort of roughly
outlines Manhattan.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:05.206
And you can sort of
see Central Park.

00:06:05.206 --> 00:06:06.830
And that's where most
of the taxi rides

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:08.550
are in this data set.

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:12.080
So that gives you a quick sense
of how I can use the notebook

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:15.320
to understand my data and to
know what sort of preprocessing

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:18.000
I might need to do.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.250
I can also go ahead
and build features.

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:25.670
Like, for example, I can use
the pick-up date and time

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:28.400
to construct two
features-- day of the week

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.302
and hour of the day.

00:06:30.302 --> 00:06:31.760
And these are much
more interesting

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:33.301
from a machine
learning point of view

00:06:33.301 --> 00:06:38.030
to understand and generalize
across different rides.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:41.690
Once again, I can execute the
query and get a data frame.

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:44.960
I can get a preview of the
data in that data frame.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:46.820
I can also get some
basic statistics,

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:51.527
like, for example, the
count of rows I got,

00:06:51.527 --> 00:06:52.610
a number of unique values.

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:55.250
So this here, I see I have
seven unique days, which is--

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:57.220
I expect that.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:00.000
I can also see minimum
and maximum values,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.090
mean, those types of things.

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:04.670
I can also do simple things
like understand the correlation

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:06.830
between fare amount, which
is the column that I'm

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:10.230
going to predict, with other
columns, for example, distance.

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:13.750
And as you can see, it's highly
correlated with distance--

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:15.420
again, something
that we would expect,

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:19.650
but good to validate from does
the data match the domain?

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:21.810
So with that, my
next step is going

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:23.686
to be around data preparation.

00:07:23.686 --> 00:07:26.060
And this is about taking your
data, producing sample data

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:30.330
sets, as well as producing
training data and evaluation

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:31.170
data.

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:32.700
So again, I'm using BigQuery.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.040
And it's a similar
query, where I'm

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:38.070
picking out the features
that I want, filtering out

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:40.860
all of the noise--

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:46.800
the location, as well as making
sure these were valid rides.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.010
And then I'm going ahead
and producing two data sets.

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:53.800
So here's my data set that
produces the evaluation set.

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:57.790
And here's my data set that
produces the training data set.

00:07:57.790 --> 00:08:01.570
I'm going to go ahead and
execute these and export them

00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:03.070
as a CSV file.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:06.280
Once these are in CSV file
in Google Cloud Storage,

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:09.320
I can then use them in
my machine learning job.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:11.500
And as a preview,
I can also look at,

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:13.810
once I've executed
this, here's the couple

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:17.850
of files that were
produced in my data set.

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:20.680
And here's a preview of
the content inside that.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:23.110
Basically, it's the
fare and a whole bunch

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:27.190
of features that are going to
be used to predict that fare.

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:31.060
This is just a good way to
get a sense of your data

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:33.520
before you start
building a model.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:35.890
It's also good to make sure
your training and evaluation

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:40.260
data are built out of the same
query so that they're in sync.

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:45.940
So let's look at going
into building a model.

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:48.190
There's traditional
models, like linear models.

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:50.680
And these fall into a
category called wide models.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:53.630
There's the new up-and-coming
neural networks,

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:57.700
deep neural networks,
which are also

00:08:57.700 --> 00:08:59.890
quite interesting
in the sense of they

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:02.470
are able to infer and learn
about the relationship

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:04.150
between your features.

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:07.210
And then there's a category
that combines the best of both.

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:09.080
And they're called
wide and deep models.

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:11.590
And that's what we're
going to go build today.

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:13.180
The inputs for this
particular model

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:14.846
are going to be the
pick-up and drop-off

00:09:14.846 --> 00:09:17.200
locations, the timestamp,
and the number of passengers.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:19.330
Think about this as the
types of information

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:24.280
you would have when trying to
estimate a fare for a ride that

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:26.970
hasn't happened yet.

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:29.250
So here's my TensorFlow code.

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:32.340
I have this already in
the interest of time.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:34.830
TensorFlow offers
a high-level API

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:40.530
called estimators, which
provide all the necessary logic

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:44.160
to build TensorFlow models
based on a given set of features

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:46.180
and set of hyper-parameters.

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:49.620
So what I have here is I'm
building a DNN-linear combined

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:51.960
regressor-- that's the
wide and deep model--

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.340
for doing regression.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:57.460
I'm predicting a price, which
is a continuous function.

00:09:57.460 --> 00:09:59.070
And so that's a
regression model.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.140
And I'm providing it a
set of linear features

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:04.650
and a set of deep features.

00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:07.330
And then here's my hidden units,
which is the number of layers.

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:10.060
And that's a
hyper-parameter to my model.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:12.660
Here's my columns.

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:16.470
I have my locations, day of
the week, hour of the day,

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:17.870
passenger count.

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:23.340
And then my deep columns are,
again, the similar columns.

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:26.160
And the deep part
of this model is

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:29.880
going to learn the relationships
between these features.

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:32.430
I can also add
features that weren't

00:10:32.430 --> 00:10:35.070
in my original data set but
synthesized from my data

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:36.600
using TensorFlow.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:39.570
So, for example, I'm
computing the distance

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:42.660
between the pick-up and
drop-off points right

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:44.170
in the TensorFlow model.

00:10:44.170 --> 00:10:46.980
And so this logic will
run both during training

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:49.710
as well as in prediction
once this model is ready.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:51.151
So once I have my
TensorFlow code,

00:10:51.151 --> 00:10:52.650
it's actually fairly
straightforward

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:54.690
to start using it.

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:57.060
Most developer
workflows are going

00:10:57.060 --> 00:10:59.820
to involve using
your sample data,

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:04.770
and training locally, and
making sure your model generally

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:06.160
works.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:10.690
So what I have here is
a gcloud command, gcloud

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:15.810
ml-engine local train, where
I point it at my Python code

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:19.680
that I was just showing, along
with references to my data.

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:23.400
So I'm using my sample
data set as well as

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:26.280
the number of steps I want
to perform training over.

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:27.540
I've already run this.

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:31.200
But what I will go
ahead and do is launch.

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:38.087
Actually, I guess
I haven't run this.

00:11:38.087 --> 00:11:39.420
So I will go ahead and run this.

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:50.980
So while this is running, I can
start bringing up TensorBoard.

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:54.760
And we can look at what happens
as the training progresses.

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:04.290
So you'll see that this is--

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:06.810
the training process
is working well.

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:08.580
It's bringing up some data.

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:10.030
We can also look at the graph.

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:14.250
This is the graph of the model
that the estimator built.

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:16.800
It has the DNN, the Deep
Neural Network part,

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:18.660
as well as the
linear model part.

00:12:21.894 --> 00:12:24.610
Let's refresh this.

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:27.070
And you can see that
as training proceeds,

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:31.930
it will incrementally
improve the model over time.

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:33.190
And you can see, it's live.

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:34.480
It's updating.

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:37.732
And we've set this to
train over 5,000 steps.

00:12:37.732 --> 00:12:39.190
It's gone about a
third of the way.

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:44.940
So we'll let this
complete in a second.

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:56.190
So meanwhile, we'll go
back to this notebook.

00:12:56.190 --> 00:13:00.890
And we'll prepare some sample
data that we'll predict over.

00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:05.915
And this is basically
the types of instances

00:13:05.915 --> 00:13:07.910
that will be used once
the model is ready.

00:13:12.460 --> 00:13:14.462
Let's see if it's done.

00:13:14.462 --> 00:13:16.420
Now, the model that's
going to be produced here

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:22.870
is actually going to be
fairly simplistic as well

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:24.677
as not very predictive,
because I'm only

00:13:24.677 --> 00:13:26.260
using a very small
