00:13:34.339 --> 00:13:35.880
So in the interest
of time, I'm going

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:37.713
to go ahead and switch
over to the next step

00:13:37.713 --> 00:13:40.680
while this is still running,
which is training on the cloud.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:45.570
Now, that's where ML Engine
shows up and helps you actually

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:49.310
use much more of your
data in the cloud.

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:52.250
So once again, it's the
same familiar command.

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:56.110
I'm using gcloud
ml-engine jobs submit

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:57.850
and submitting a training job.

00:13:57.850 --> 00:14:01.210
And here, many of
the parameters are

00:14:01.210 --> 00:14:03.045
the same as the
local case, where I'm

00:14:03.045 --> 00:14:05.440
pointing it at my Python code.

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:06.867
But now, in the
context of Cloud,

00:14:06.867 --> 00:14:08.950
I'm also telling it where
in Cloud, which region--

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:12.070
for example, US Central--

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:14.090
which directory to
put the outputs into,

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:18.100
which is going to be a
Google Cloud Storage bucket.

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:21.710
And then I'm also going
to pick a scale tier.

00:14:21.710 --> 00:14:23.710
And here, I've picked a
scale tier called basic,

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:26.200
which is basically a
single, no-training job.

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:29.860
And once I execute that,
this job will get submitted.

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:33.010
I can go to the
console and open up--

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:38.970
so I've already run this job.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:42.270
You can see, the
console shows this job.

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:44.520
You can also look at the
logs for this particular job.

00:14:50.510 --> 00:14:54.860
And this shows all of the logs,
including things like the loss

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:57.250
and the global steps as
the training progressed,

00:14:57.250 --> 00:14:59.210
all outputted in a
log for you to be

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:00.500
able to see what happened.

00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:15.110
Like the local case, I can
also bring up TensorBoard

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:18.045
and open that.

00:15:18.045 --> 00:15:19.920
And this particular job
is already completed.

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:24.080
So you can see,
this particular job

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:26.150
has made it all the way through.

00:15:26.150 --> 00:15:28.580
I can see the loss,
the global steps

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:31.220
all progressed to
the 5,000 steps

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:35.010
that I chose for this
Cloud job as well.

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:37.160
So that's great.

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:42.960
I can go ahead and also use
GPUs on the cloud very easily.

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:45.080
So this is the same job.

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:49.460
All I did was change scale
tier from basic to basic GPU.

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:53.570
And now I have a single
no-training job using GPUs

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:56.370
without having to
worry about setting up

00:15:56.370 --> 00:16:00.830
GPUs or software
associated with GPUs.

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:02.019
And I can do the same thing.

00:16:02.019 --> 00:16:02.810
It'll launch a job.

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:06.320
I can go to the console, look
at logs, look at TensorBoard.

00:16:06.320 --> 00:16:08.750
More interesting in
this particular example

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:10.460
is distributor training.

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:15.020
So very similar, again,
all I needed to do

00:16:15.020 --> 00:16:18.500
was change the scale tier
from basic to standard.

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:21.350
And now this gives me a
configuration, a cluster,

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:26.750
where I have a master node that
runs training and evaluation,

00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:29.000
a set of workers, and a
set of parameter servers

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:32.360
that share a state
for the whole job.

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:34.610
And what this thing
does is, it allows

00:16:34.610 --> 00:16:38.150
me to use a lot more of
my data and put a lot more

00:16:38.150 --> 00:16:39.770
compute power behind it.

00:16:39.770 --> 00:16:42.520
So let me open up a
TensorBoard for this.

00:16:52.195 --> 00:16:53.570
So it's getting
the same set of--

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:55.492
similar set of charts.

00:16:55.492 --> 00:16:57.860
But an interesting
thing to look at here

00:16:57.860 --> 00:16:59.690
is global steps per second.

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:04.730
And what I'm seeing is something
like 55 steps per second.

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:07.099
Worse is, say, the
other job, where

00:17:07.099 --> 00:17:10.751
I had a single node, where
I was getting about 14.

00:17:10.751 --> 00:17:12.500
And so this is sort
of the power of Cloud,

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:14.810
where I was able to spin
up a cluster very quickly

00:17:14.810 --> 00:17:19.750
and easily for my job and be
able to increase the throughput

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:22.319
at which my training happens.

00:17:22.319 --> 00:17:25.445
So now, let's also look
at what this job produced.

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:32.910
As output, all of
the model files

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:35.072
were produced into
Google Cloud Storage.

00:17:35.072 --> 00:17:41.520
So this is where this particular
job saved all the outputs.

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:45.630
I have my checkpoints, which are
intermediate stages of my model

00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:47.580
as the training is progressing.

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:50.190
In case the job needs
to recover and restart,

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:53.370
it can pick up from the
last known checkpoint.

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:54.945
It also produces
all of the events.

00:17:58.387 --> 00:18:02.990
These summary files are the ones
that TensorBoard visualizes.

00:18:02.990 --> 00:18:06.140
These contain a log of all of
the metrics that were produced.

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:09.810
But most importantly, you
have the model itself.

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:15.050
So here is my saved model
and the set of variables

00:18:15.050 --> 00:18:16.520
that were saved along with it.

00:18:16.520 --> 00:18:18.672
These variables are the
learned weights, so all

00:18:18.672 --> 00:18:20.630
of the weights of the
linear parts of the model

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:22.670
as well as the deep
parts of the model.

00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:26.132
And this is what I will
use to perform predictions.

00:18:26.132 --> 00:18:27.590
So in order to
perform predictions,

00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:29.464
I'll go ahead and actually
deploy this model.

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:37.240
So I'm going to go ahead and
refer to that model using

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:42.490
that path in Cloud Storage where
the saved model was located.

00:18:42.490 --> 00:18:45.220
And I'm going to
issue two commands.

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:46.840
ml-engine models
create-- this creates

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:49.700
a new model called "taxifare."

00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:51.820
And then I could
go ahead and deploy

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:57.090
a particular version of the
model using what I just saved.

00:18:57.090 --> 00:18:58.980
And if I go to my console--

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:02.230
I've already gone ahead
and saved this out.

00:19:02.230 --> 00:19:05.970
So here's my taxifare model.

00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:10.230
And it has a v1 model that
I've already gone ahead

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:12.670
and deployed.

00:19:12.670 --> 00:19:16.490
Once I have a model deployed,
I'm ready to use it.

00:19:16.490 --> 00:19:18.720
Here's a little
bit of Python code

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:22.630
to issue a HTTP request
to this particular model.

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:24.520
So here's where you can see--

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:27.630
here's the URL for
this particular model.

00:19:27.630 --> 00:19:29.880
As a developer, I
did not have to write

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:33.780
any of the associated
HTTP application code.

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:36.000
All I did was deploy
the model that

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:37.710
was produced from my training.

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:43.050
And I can go ahead and construct
an instance with the features

00:19:43.050 --> 00:19:46.620
that I want to predict
over, issue the response--

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:49.320
issue the request, and
receive a response.

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:52.176
And the response is, as you
might expect, the prediction.

00:19:52.176 --> 00:19:53.550
And in this
particular case, it's

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:56.760
predicting that the
fare is going to be $11.

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:59.760
So as you can see,
I went very quickly

00:19:59.760 --> 00:20:03.397
from using TensorFlow, training
a model locally, building

00:20:03.397 --> 00:20:04.605
and training a model locally.

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:08.960
With a single
configuration change,

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:11.070
I was able to train it on Cloud.

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:16.350
Without having to worry about
compute VMs and resources

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:20.400
or job management, I was able
to go from CPU-only training

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:25.800
to GPUs as well as from single
node to distributed training.

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:27.760
And then finally, once
I have a model ready,

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:32.820
I can go ahead and deploy it
as a version and try it out.

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:37.470
And this API is something that I
could bring into my application

00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:40.590
and embed it in my
app to be able to use

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:42.424
this model in production.

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:48.110
So that was a very
quick overview

00:20:48.110 --> 00:20:50.600
of ML Engine in action.

00:20:53.740 --> 00:20:58.030
ML Engine is part of a
much larger set of products

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:00.550
that are geared
around helping you

00:21:00.550 --> 00:21:02.711
with your entire
end-to-end ML workflow.

00:21:02.711 --> 00:21:04.210
And this ranges
from data injection,

00:21:04.210 --> 00:21:08.320
bringing new data into Cloud,
to managing and storing it--

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:12.430
things like Google Cloud
Storage as well as BigQuery--

00:21:12.430 --> 00:21:15.460
exploration-- Datalab--
you saw that--

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:18.670
as well as the power
of SQL and BigQuery.

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:21.970
And then you saw ML Engine
doing the modeling work.

00:21:21.970 --> 00:21:25.180
And then beyond this sort of
single development workflow,

00:21:25.180 --> 00:21:28.270
we also have products to
allow you to build pipelines

00:21:28.270 --> 00:21:32.860
that allow you to construct
models over data as it arrives

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:37.900
or over time and updating your
models in an automated way.

00:21:37.900 --> 00:21:41.120
And so this is sort of
from data, to exploration,

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:42.898
to training, to production.

00:21:45.530 --> 00:21:49.960
So with that, to recap,
we have TensorFlow, which

00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:52.030
you can find on tensorflow.org.

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:56.500
This is Google's machine
learning runtime,

00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:59.410
with the latest
and greatest APIs.

00:21:59.410 --> 00:22:02.410
You can use ML Engine to
train and predict at scale.

00:22:02.410 --> 00:22:04.300
And you can use familiar
Jupyter Notebooks

00:22:04.300 --> 00:22:09.500
using Cloud Datalab for
your data science workflows.

00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:15.980
We're going to switch
over to questions.

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:17.360
We're going to
take a quick break

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:21.590
and then come back to live Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in less than a

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:22.828
minute

00:25:02.941 --> 00:25:03.440
All right.

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:06.390
We're back with questions
from the audience.

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:08.300
This is great to
see, that people

00:25:08.300 --> 00:25:10.970
have provided their questions.

00:25:10.970 --> 00:25:13.040
And I'm happy to take
more questions as we go

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:16.700
along answering the first few.

00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:18.480
"Since the model is
as good as the data,

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:22.640
how do I tag a model with the
specific versions of data?"

00:25:22.640 --> 00:25:26.560
So yes, indeed, machine
learning is largely--

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:28.100
how good your model
is is certainly

00:25:28.100 --> 00:25:30.920
based on the quality
of your data.

00:25:30.920 --> 00:25:34.160
And in the experiment
scenario, you're

00:25:34.160 --> 00:25:37.280
going to try out your model
with different data sets.

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:45.050
One of the ways you might manage
your data is based on dates

00:25:45.050 --> 00:25:48.490
and partition your
data along with dates.

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:51.740
And you can use
those dates as a way

00:25:51.740 --> 00:25:55.770
to generate a version in
your training program.

00:25:55.770 --> 00:25:58.130
And as part of
producing a model,

00:25:58.130 --> 00:26:00.920
you can also, alongside
the model, the saved model

00:26:00.920 --> 00:26:04.850
that I was showing, also
output some metadata

00:26:04.850 --> 00:26:08.000
about the data that was
used, in particular, things

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:09.480
like the date.

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:12.470
And at time of
deployment, you can

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:16.320
take those pieces of metadata
and produce a version number.

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:20.000
So, for example, if a model
was trained over the last seven

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:23.990
days, I can take
today's date as an input

00:26:23.990 --> 00:26:27.060
into producing the version.

00:26:27.060 --> 00:26:28.430
That's just one strategy.

00:26:28.430 --> 00:26:33.320
You might have different labels
for your data set, for example,

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:36.830
like a production set versus
a experimental data set.

00:26:36.830 --> 00:26:42.050
And you might actually have
names that you use your--

00:26:42.050 --> 00:26:44.330
that you use to manage
your catalog of data.

00:26:44.330 --> 00:26:47.240
And that would be
another piece of input

00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:49.520
that you might use
in constructing

00:26:49.520 --> 00:26:52.270
the version of your model.

00:26:52.270 --> 00:26:55.340
ML Engine allows you to
deploy multiple models as well

00:26:55.340 --> 00:26:57.020
as multiple versions per model.

00:26:57.020 --> 00:27:00.250
So you can actually have, for
example, my taxifare model,

00:27:00.250 --> 00:27:01.540
and I can be have v1.

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:04.560
I can have v2 and 3 and so on.

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:07.550
I can also have
something like latest.

00:27:07.550 --> 00:27:11.420
I can declare one of them
as latest or default.

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:14.450
Or I can be explicit about
the version I want to go use.

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:16.880
So that's a sort
of overview of how

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:21.230
I might do versioning
and managing

00:27:21.230 --> 00:27:24.410
the source of data that was used
to produce a particular version

00:27:24.410 --> 00:27:26.070
of the model.

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:28.760
Moving on to the next question--

00:27:28.760 --> 00:27:30.570
"Will training with ML
Engine show how long

00:27:30.570 --> 00:27:33.285
training is estimated to take
so that I can estimate costs?"

00:27:36.510 --> 00:27:39.120
It's actually quite
interesting and challenging

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:42.905
to offer an estimate of exactly
how long any particular job is

00:27:42.905 --> 00:27:44.280
going to take,
because it largely

00:27:44.280 --> 00:27:47.040
is a function of
your code in terms

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:49.590
of how complex the
TensorFlow graph is,

00:27:49.590 --> 00:27:52.230
the kinds of operations
it uses, as well

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:55.480
as the data that you have.

00:27:55.480 --> 00:28:00.420
There's a variety of
numbers that matter-- things

00:28:00.420 --> 00:28:05.410
like the batch size, how much
data you read in every batch.

00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:10.140
So the best way to
estimate a training job

00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:15.870
is to run it, and in
the first few minutes,

00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:19.200
look at the throughput and
see how many steps you're

00:28:19.200 --> 00:28:21.460
progressing per second.

00:28:21.460 --> 00:28:24.240
And you can use that
as a rough measure

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:27.390
to estimate how long it's going
to take for the entire job

00:28:27.390 --> 00:28:29.640
to run through.

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:32.250
So that's probably the
best way to get a sense.

00:28:32.250 --> 00:28:34.890
And you can tweak the
parameters and see

00:28:34.890 --> 00:28:37.620
how they have a bearing
on your particular problem

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:41.474
and the costs associated
with that, correspondingly.

00:28:45.110 --> 00:28:48.490
So next question-- "Is ML
Engine TensorFlow only?

00:28:48.490 --> 00:28:51.010
I use sklearn"--
or scikit-learn.

00:28:51.010 --> 00:28:53.290
"What can you do for Me ?"

00:28:53.290 --> 00:28:57.100
So indeed, what I showed today
was ML Engine with TensorFlow.

00:28:57.100 --> 00:29:00.460
And ML Engine v1 was
certainly around TensorFlow.

00:29:00.460 --> 00:29:02.590
And this has been--

00:29:02.590 --> 00:29:06.460
you can see a lot of
momentum around TensorFlow.

00:29:06.460 --> 00:29:08.496
As Google open-sourced
TensorFlow,

00:29:08.496 --> 00:29:10.870
it's quickly become one of
the most popular deep learning

00:29:10.870 --> 00:29:12.040
frameworks.

00:29:12.040 --> 00:29:15.340
And we've continued to
take advantage of that

00:29:15.340 --> 00:29:17.500
and bring you all of
the power of TensorFlow,

00:29:17.500 --> 00:29:20.650
whether it's accelerated
training, distributed training,

00:29:20.650 --> 00:29:25.430
and shortly, TPUs as well.

00:29:25.430 --> 00:29:28.460
However, we definitely
have heard from customers.

00:29:28.460 --> 00:29:34.810
And we want to support a vast
and comprehensive toolbox

00:29:34.810 --> 00:29:37.150
of ML capabilities.

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:39.070
And that includes
things like sklearn.

00:29:39.070 --> 00:29:41.459
It includes things like
XGBoost, as well as

00:29:41.459 --> 00:29:43.750
potentially any other framework
that you want to bring.

00:29:43.750 --> 00:29:47.590
And so this is something that
I'd ask you to stay tuned for.

00:29:47.590 --> 00:29:54.850
This is shortly forthcoming,
starting with on the prediction

00:29:54.850 --> 00:29:58.210
side, where you can train a
model wherever you run training

00:29:58.210 --> 00:30:02.570
today and bring it to Cloud
to put it into production.

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:05.170
And then we'll follow that
with training as well.

00:30:08.140 --> 00:30:09.220
Moving right along.

00:30:09.220 --> 00:30:12.260
"When can I get GPUs and
TPUs to train faster?"

00:30:12.260 --> 00:30:13.450
So GPUs are available today.

00:30:13.450 --> 00:30:14.560
I actually used it.

00:30:14.560 --> 00:30:16.570
And I showed how you
can get started using it

00:30:16.570 --> 00:30:19.240
with a simple
configuration that says,

00:30:19.240 --> 00:30:21.670
use a GPU-enabled cluster.

00:30:21.670 --> 00:30:26.590
We have both Akaze as
well as Pascal P100s.

00:30:26.590 --> 00:30:29.480
You can also get not
just single-GPU training,

00:30:29.480 --> 00:30:33.580
but you can also get two
and four for every worker.

00:30:33.580 --> 00:30:35.920
And a lot of people
actually use a single node

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:39.560
with multiple GPUs to really
scale up their training.

00:30:39.560 --> 00:30:42.060
TPUs are around the corner.

00:30:42.060 --> 00:30:44.200
We have a product
called Cloud TPU,

00:30:44.200 --> 00:30:50.560
which is the infrastructure as
a service offering with GCE.

00:30:50.560 --> 00:30:54.340
And so you could
spin up a TPU node

00:30:54.340 --> 00:30:56.770
and use that to
get TPU training.

00:30:56.770 --> 00:30:59.350
With ML Engine,
you'll see, again,

00:30:59.350 --> 00:31:03.760
a very managed experience, where
you can easily opt in into TPU,

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:05.470
just like I did for GPUs.

00:31:05.470 --> 00:31:08.770
And ML engine will take
care of all of the resource

00:31:08.770 --> 00:31:11.687
provisioning and managing
that through the lifecycle

00:31:11.687 --> 00:31:12.520
of the training job.

00:31:15.040 --> 00:31:18.580
We'd love to, of course,
hear your interests.

00:31:18.580 --> 00:31:21.730
Please contact us
through the feedback link

00:31:21.730 --> 00:31:25.570
that we have on our
Docs page in terms

00:31:25.570 --> 00:31:29.050
of expressing your interest
to sign up for a TPU preview.

00:31:32.020 --> 00:31:34.070
So moving to the next question--

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:35.720
"TensorFlow models seem complex.

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:38.460
Where do I start for my image
classification problem?"

00:31:38.460 --> 00:31:41.490
So let me answer
that in two parts.

00:31:41.490 --> 00:31:48.020
I will switch to my demo code.

00:31:48.020 --> 00:31:53.570
So TensorFlow models can
be complex in the sense

00:31:53.570 --> 00:31:56.570
that they allow you to do
new and interesting things

00:31:56.570 --> 00:31:58.220
and use the full
power of TensorFlow.

00:31:58.220 --> 00:32:00.110
However, if you're
doing basic things,

00:32:00.110 --> 00:32:04.190
like a linear regression
model, TensorFlow

00:32:04.190 --> 00:32:10.050
has a number of APIs that will
help you get started easily.

00:32:10.050 --> 00:32:12.614
So I'm actually going to
show you on my screen--

00:32:12.614 --> 00:32:14.030
go back and look
a little bit more

00:32:14.030 --> 00:32:16.160
closely at this
particular piece of code.

00:32:38.037 --> 00:32:40.370
So what I've gone ahead and
done is brought back my code

00:32:40.370 --> 00:32:41.775
on screen.

00:32:41.775 --> 00:32:44.150
And let me walk you through
a little bit more of the code

00:32:44.150 --> 00:32:45.857
that I had for my
particular model.

00:32:45.857 --> 00:32:47.690
So I'm going ahead and
importing TensorFlow,

00:32:47.690 --> 00:32:53.360
which is, of course, the
standard TensorFlow package.

00:32:53.360 --> 00:32:55.190
And here's the estimator API.

00:32:55.190 --> 00:32:59.420
So the estimator API lives
under an estimator module.

00:32:59.420 --> 00:33:01.400
And it has a number
of estimators.

00:33:01.400 --> 00:33:04.220
DNN linear combined is
the one that I used.

00:33:04.220 --> 00:33:06.920
There's DNN, there's
linear, and there's

00:33:06.920 --> 00:33:09.020
more that are coming up.

00:33:09.020 --> 00:33:11.690
And these provide a
simplified interface

00:33:11.690 --> 00:33:16.250
to all of the power of
TensorFlow by a high-level API.

00:33:16.250 --> 00:33:18.230
Once I've constructed
my estimator,

00:33:18.230 --> 00:33:21.710
I also go ahead and
construct my data set.

00:33:21.710 --> 00:33:23.330
So here, I'm
constructing a data set

00:33:23.330 --> 00:33:28.160
that's a text line data set,
because I'm using CSV files.

00:33:28.160 --> 00:33:30.560
And I can very
easily say, I would

00:33:30.560 --> 00:33:34.670
like to repeat over that
data set for a given

00:33:34.670 --> 00:33:37.874
number of epochs and read
that in a batched fashion

00:33:37.874 --> 00:33:39.540
without actually
having to deal with all

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:43.735
of the low-level APIs in
TensorFlow that are there

00:33:43.735 --> 00:33:46.970
and offer a lot of
power, but I don't

00:33:46.970 --> 00:33:49.280
have to use them in
this particular case.

00:33:49.280 --> 00:33:52.010
Once I've constructed
my estimator,

00:33:52.010 --> 00:33:55.220
I can go ahead and construct
a training specification

00:33:55.220 --> 00:33:56.790
with that data set.

00:33:56.790 --> 00:33:59.060
I can go ahead and construct
an eval specification

00:33:59.060 --> 00:34:02.290
and then simply go ahead
and call train and evaluate.

00:34:02.290 --> 00:34:03.710
And so this gives
me a simple way

00:34:03.710 --> 00:34:05.750
to get started with TensorFlow.

00:34:05.750 --> 00:34:10.329
Now, the question was also
about image classification.

00:34:10.329 --> 00:34:11.870
Now, image classification
is actually

00:34:11.870 --> 00:34:13.881
quite interesting
in that there's

00:34:13.881 --> 00:34:15.380
a technique called
transfer learning

00:34:15.380 --> 00:34:17.247
that you might actually use.

00:34:17.247 --> 00:34:18.830
And this is one of
the samples that is

00:34:18.830 --> 00:34:20.120
available with Cloud Datalab.

00:34:20.120 --> 00:34:23.420
If you were to go
get that, there's

00:34:23.420 --> 00:34:26.504
some prebuilt samples
that cover that use case.

00:34:26.504 --> 00:34:27.920
The idea with
transfer learning is

00:34:27.920 --> 00:34:30.889
you take a image
model that's been

00:34:30.889 --> 00:34:35.840
trained over a very large
number of general purpose

00:34:35.840 --> 00:34:37.100
set of images.

00:34:37.100 --> 00:34:39.620
And then you take that as a
starting point for your model

00:34:39.620 --> 00:34:42.110
and fine-tune that
model with a smaller

00:34:42.110 --> 00:34:44.300
set of more
domain-specific images

00:34:44.300 --> 00:34:47.420
for your particular problem
and the particular set

00:34:47.420 --> 00:34:51.280
of labels that you're trying
to classify those images over.

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:56.929
And so that gets you started
with not just prebuilt models

00:34:56.929 --> 00:35:01.965
for TensorFlow, but also
pretrained models that

00:35:01.965 --> 00:35:03.840
have been trained over
a very large data set.

00:35:09.180 --> 00:35:11.090
So there's more questions
that have come in.

00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:18.610
The question says, "The
deep part of the model

00:35:18.610 --> 00:35:21.830
prepares the weight matrix
to be applied in prediction,

00:35:21.830 --> 00:35:24.682
which is done by the wide."

00:35:24.682 --> 00:35:26.140
So in reality,
what's happening is,

00:35:26.140 --> 00:35:29.680
the model consists of-- the
model architecture consists

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:30.700
of two parts--

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:35.560
a wide part that is similar
to linear regression

00:35:35.560 --> 00:35:39.280
and a deep part that's
similar to DNNs.

00:35:39.280 --> 00:35:41.860
And both of these are
used at training time

00:35:41.860 --> 00:35:43.660
as well as at prediction time.

00:35:43.660 --> 00:35:45.760
It's the same graph
and the same set

00:35:45.760 --> 00:35:49.480
of features that are being used
in both parts of the model.

00:35:49.480 --> 00:35:52.975
And as the models--

00:35:55.850 --> 00:35:58.810
as the ports of
the graph execute,

00:35:58.810 --> 00:36:02.860
their outputs are combined to
produce the final regression

00:36:02.860 --> 00:36:03.970
or classification.

00:36:03.970 --> 00:36:06.340
It depends on the
model that you have.

00:36:06.340 --> 00:36:08.558
So both are used at prediction.

00:36:11.790 --> 00:36:12.630
So moving on.

00:36:12.630 --> 00:36:14.760
"How do I pipeline
model inputs--

00:36:14.760 --> 00:36:17.490
features and vectors--
and evaluate performance

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:21.760
and its accuracy in TensorFlow?"

00:36:21.760 --> 00:36:26.710
So I'm assuming that
pipeline refers to--

00:36:26.710 --> 00:36:29.080
I have a pipeline of--

00:36:29.080 --> 00:36:37.270
a larger pipeline that goes from
my raw data to preprocessed.

00:36:37.270 --> 00:36:39.260
So this is the part
that I would do

00:36:39.260 --> 00:36:41.260
in one of the data
processing technologies

00:36:41.260 --> 00:36:42.490
that Google Cloud has.

00:36:42.490 --> 00:36:44.110
So this might be BigQuery.

00:36:44.110 --> 00:36:46.316
Or it might be
something like Dataflow.

00:36:46.316 --> 00:36:47.690
Or if you're
familiar with Spark,

00:36:47.690 --> 00:36:50.080
you could use Cloud Dataproc.

00:36:50.080 --> 00:36:53.860
And these data
processing tools would

00:36:53.860 --> 00:36:56.560
allow you to take your
data, the raw data,

00:36:56.560 --> 00:36:59.130
and convert those
into feature rectors.

00:37:02.260 --> 00:37:05.920
Like I did, you would build both
training and evaluation sets

00:37:05.920 --> 00:37:10.930
and use the evaluation set to
compute a set of predictions.

00:37:10.930 --> 00:37:12.430
Once you have those
predictions, you

00:37:12.430 --> 00:37:16.000
can now compare them
against the expected values

00:37:16.000 --> 00:37:19.610
and get a sense of the
accuracy of the model.

00:37:23.300 --> 00:37:24.799
"Since a model is as good"--

00:37:24.799 --> 00:37:26.090
I think we've covered this one.

